1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,980
But the name stuck, right?

2
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:01,560
The name stuck.

3
00:00:01,980 --> 00:00:02,380
Maisie's Galaxy.

4
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:07,160
With an image, you can really just have a guess at how far away a galaxy is,

5
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:11,340
and it was spectroscopically confirmed. Like, it's real.

6
00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:14,480
It's really far away, and it remains one of the first—

7
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:19,380
there are now several other results showing galaxies that are brighter and at further

8
00:00:19,380 --> 00:00:24,420
distances—but it's one of the first real challenges to our understanding of how you

9
00:00:24,420 --> 00:00:25,240
can form galaxies.

10
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:28,780
How do you get Maisie's Galaxy that early in the universe?

11
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:32,040
It means that the star formation had to have started earlier.

12
00:00:32,300 --> 00:00:34,160
It had to have been more efficient.

13
00:00:34,720 --> 00:00:40,380
The universe had to be much better at collapsing gas into stars than we thought before JWST,

14
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:48,020
which is really exciting. Because I think the coolest, or the best, like, homage, I guess,

15
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:51,100
to a telescope, is that you have to redefine what you know.

16
00:00:52,520 --> 00:00:56,560
The observations you get from it mean you have to redefine what you know about physics.

17
00:00:56,560 --> 00:00:57,880
And that's really cool.

